/*!
    \page qwe-releases.html

    \title Qt WebEngine and Qt Pdf Releases
    \brief Information about Qt WebEngine and Qt Pdf versioning and release schedules.

    \section1 Qt and Chromium combined

    Qt WebEngine and Qt Pdf, also abbreviated as "QWE", consists of the API layer and
    adaptation layer for the Qt framework surrounding a specific version of Chromium.

    Starting with Qt 6.8, Qt WebEngine was provided as a separate download
    outside of the main selection tree for Qt framework in the Qt Maintenance
    Tool. With Qt 6.12, the Qt WebEngine source code is decoupled as a
    sub-module from the Qt super-repository on Git, see
    \l{https://www.qt.io/blog/qt-webengine-will-release-separate}{Qt WebEngine Separate Release}.

    The goal for these separations is to gain more flexibility in releasing Qt
    WebEngine, and make releases more focused on security patches in Chromium
    and less driven by Qt Framework release cadence.

    There is another Qt Extensions module which uses Chromium as a 3rd party
    dependency: \l [QtPDF]{Qt Pdf}. Qt Pdf uses \l {https://pdfium.googlesource.com/}{PDFium},
    which is an open-source C++ PDF rendering engine embedded directly into the
    Chromium project. The below sections cover the releasing of \l [QtPDF]{Qt Pdf}
    as well, even if not explicitly mentioning it.

    Note that a release of Qt WebEngine also contain the Qt WebView plugin for Qt
    WebEngine.

    \section1 The main baseline and intake of security patches

    Integrations of Chromium releases in Qt WebEngine follow two baselines. The
    \l{https://www.chromium.org/developers/version-numbers/}{Major version of Chromium}
    used as the main baseline is listed as "Based on" in the first line of
    the file \c CHROMIUM_VERSION in the root folder of the Qt WebEngine
    repository, for example, like here:

    \badcode
    Based on Chromium version: 140.0.7339.264
    Patched with security patches up to Chromium version: 156.0.8078.74
    \endcode


    This "Based on" version brings new features and other essential changes in
    Chromium that are important for Qt WebEngine.

    According to the Chromium release scheme (see
    \l {https://chromiumdash.appspot.com/schedule}{Chromium release schedule}),
    the number "140" refers to the generation of Chromium releases as a main
    branch. It is used as a reference in Qt WebEngine for the main baseline of
    Chromium.

    The "Patched with..." version on second line in the file \c CHROMIUM_VERSION
    lists the last version of Chromium used as a source of security patches.
    This line forms the security baseline of Chromium for Qt WebEngine, "74" in the
    above example. This number is important for users since it marks the integration
    point in Chromium with the most recent security patches.

    Even though the goal is to integrate as many security patches as possible,
    not all patches are taken in along a security baseline. Some of them are not
    relevant for the Qt WebEngine API, while others have a low security ranking
    and are safe to be taken in later, during the next main baseline upgrade of
    Chromium.

    \section1 Versioning

    Like the Qt framework, Qt WebEngine uses \l{https://semver.org/}{semantic versioning}, for instance, \c {"6.140.0"}.

    Major releases increment the major version number, resetting the minor
    version to zero. This number follows the major version of the Qt framework
    with which this Qt WebEngine is compatible. Here, it is Qt 6. Compared to the
    Qt framework, it does not reset the minor version number. See the next section
    for details about this.

    Minor releases increment the minor version number, resetting the patch
    version number to zero. Minor versions contain the major version of
    Chromium as well as new functionality in Qt WebEngine. The "140" in the
    above example stands for the major version of Chromium used as the main
    baseline in the series of patch releases in this minor version.

    Patch releases - also called maintenance releases - increment just the patch
    version number. Patch releases consist of bug fixes in Qt WebEngine,
    security fixes in Chromium, documentation fixes, and performance
    improvements.

    \section1 Alignment with Qt framework regular and Long-Term Support releases

    Qt WebEngine is a Qt Extension Module compatible and supported with a range
    of Qt framework releases.

    Like with Qt framework releases prior to the separation in Qt 6.12.0, a
    release of Qt WebEngine could be used with Qt until the last supported Qt
    framework Long-Term Support release. For instance, the next Qt framework
    LTS release will be Qt 6.16. A release of Qt WebEngine provided after the Qt
    6.16.0 release can also be used with Qt 6.12 LTS as well as with Qt 6.15.x,
    whereas this Qt WebEngine release will be the last for Qt 6.15.x, since Qt
    6.15 is a regular release. A list of compatible Qt versions is provided in
    the release notes file \c{release-note.md} in the root folder of the
    Qt WebEngine repository

    \section1 Release schedules

    A released version of Qt WebEngine is supported and maintained for around 3
    months, i.e., a maximum of six major versions of Chromium. After this, users
    should upgrade to the next maintained Qt WebEngine version. This migration
    is needed for Qt WebEngine to keep in-taking new security fixes and use new
    features in Chromium. The support and maintenance period for a given patch
    release of Qt WebEngine is also capped by the end of support of a Qt LTS
    release. After this point, users should upgrade to a newer Qt WebEngine and
    use a new Qt release.

    \section1 Pre-built binaries

    One path to use Qt WebEngine with different versions of the Qt framework is
    to use pre-built binaries. They are provided for the main desktop platforms
    via the Qt Maintenance Tool. They are linked in the selection tree to the
    nodes named after the Qt framework minor release they are built with.

    \section1 Making your own builds

    If pre-built binaries are not available for a version of the Qt framework,
    the user should create their own build, see \l {Qt WebEngine Platform Notes}
    {Qt WebEngine}.

    \section1 Qt Pdf in the new versioning and releasing

    All principles and rules in above sections apply to Qt Pdf as well. Certainly,
    Qt PDF is a Qt Extension on it own and brings its own API, see
    \l [QtPDF]{Qt Pdf}

    \section1 Commercial support

    Terms and scope for commercial support for Qt WebEngine are the same as for
    the Qt framework, see [link].
*/
